{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1973 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "696f9e83b140df5cb7ceadbd9c4f30d814a21e95b1eaaf801969d5925d080ffb" :edb/db-before-t 1972 :edb/db-after-t 1973 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535285 50 #inst "2026-10-13T14:02:09.859Z" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186052971 1078 #edb/ref 17592186052976 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186054659 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491186 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186054775 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491160 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186054839 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491149 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186054840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491166 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186055289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186055289 1071 "Evaluate $3 - \\frac{m}{3}$ for $m = 6$." 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186055289 1071 "Evaluate $3-\\frac{m}{3}$ for $m=6.$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186055289 1074 "Replacing $m$ with $6$ in the expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\frac{m}{3} & = \\\\ 3-\\frac{6}{3} & = \\\\ 3-2 & = \\\\ 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186058374 1078 #edb/ref 17592186058378 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186059465 1071 "What is the length of the hypotenuse in a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle if the side opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle has a length of $6\\,\\textrm{cm}$?" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186059465 1071 "What is the length of the hypotenuse in a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle if the side opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle has a length of $6\\,\\textrm{cm}?$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186059465 1074 "Let's refer to our standard $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle.\n\n![](images/5f/5fe1cd7975f1537bead1f9560816dafe5e8f3ec0db3e683e2bd3e00c4ac54506.png)\n\nIn any $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle, the hypotenuse is twice the length of the shorter leg.\n\nThe leg opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle is the shorter leg of the triangle. So here, the shorter leg has length\n\n$$\nx=6\\, \\text{cm}\n$$\n\nSo here, the length of the hypotenuse must be\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 6 = 12\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186059465 1074 "Let's refer to our standard $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle.\n\n![](images/5f/5fe1cd7975f1537bead1f9560816dafe5e8f3ec0db3e683e2bd3e00c4ac54506.png)\n\nIn any $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle, the hypotenuse is twice the length of the shorter leg.\n\nThe leg opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle is the shorter leg of the triangle. So here, the shorter leg has length $x=6\\, \\text{cm}.$\n\nSo here, the length of the hypotenuse must be\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 6 = 12\\,\\textrm{cm}.\n$$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186059467 1074 "Let's refer to our standard $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle.\n\n![](images/58/58993cc083d8561501ea9db70c6d4d886fadc4d4ac3455a1a58de1fe5ceb6e77.png)\n\nSince this is a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle, the length of the longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times that of the shorter leg.\n\nHere, the longer leg has length $2$, and the shorter leg has length $x$, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= \\sqrt{3}x \\\\\nx &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the radical from the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and denominator by the radical:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{2 \\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the length of the shorter leg is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186059467 1074 "Let's refer to our standard $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle.\n\n![](images/58/58993cc083d8561501ea9db70c6d4d886fadc4d4ac3455a1a58de1fe5ceb6e77.png)\n\nSince this is a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle, the length of the longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times that of the shorter leg.\n\nHere, the longer leg has length $2,$ and the shorter leg has length $x,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\sqrt{3}x \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the radical from the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and denominator by the radical:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the length of the shorter leg is $\\dfrac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186059468 1071 "What is the length of the longer leg in a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle if the hypotenuse has a length of $6$?" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186059468 1071 "What is the length of the longer leg in a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle if the hypotenuse has a length of $6?$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186059468 1074 "Let's refer to our standard $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle.\n\n![](images/9f/9f62659af506661e1343caef0afa5f2072298879f17dcf8a44ab610f51f1e30f.png)\n\nSince the hypotenuse has a length of $6$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $x$, we can calculate the length of the longer leg as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}x = \\sqrt{3} \\cdot 3 = 3\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the longer leg has a length of $3 \\sqrt{3}$." 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186059468 1074 "Let's refer to our standard $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle.\n\n![](images/9f/9f62659af506661e1343caef0afa5f2072298879f17dcf8a44ab610f51f1e30f.png)\n\nSince the hypotenuse has a length of $6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $x,$ we can calculate the length of the longer leg as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}x = \\sqrt{3} \\cdot 3 = 3\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the longer leg has a length of $3 \\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186059637 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491179 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186059637 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491201 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186059639 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491122 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186059640 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491146 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186059640 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491188 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186059937 1050 420.0 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186060292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186060292 1071 "![](images/9a/9aef509e210c6d64da56119083ba36d9cc1f4e9b4533cf68285806a5606881cb.png)\n\nIn the triangle above, what is the length of $AC^(―)$?" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186060292 1071 "![](images/9a/9aef509e210c6d64da56119083ba36d9cc1f4e9b4533cf68285806a5606881cb.png)\n\nIn the triangle above, what is the length of $\\overset{―}{AC}?$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186060292 1074 "Let's refer to our standard ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle.\n\n![](images/27/277e019b26f72cffd7647abe694338acd099e4d48fc532a7e9d6ad362a49dcc9.png)\n\nSince this is a ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle, the length of the longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times that of the shorter leg.\n\nHere, the shorter leg has length $x=6$ and the longer leg has length $\\sqrt{3}x,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{AC}=\\sqrt{3}x=\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 6=6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the length of the longer leg is $6\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186060874 1064 "Let's consider an equilateral triangle with sides of length $2x$. Notice that any altitude will split it into two congruent triangles, as shown below.\n\n![](images/30/301fe26a314d91d0d316758a5076cea2383f325991789addceaec71f0e7b16dd.png)\n\nIn the new smaller triangle the acute angles will be $30^\\circ$ and $60^\\circ$. Such triangles are called **$\\boldsymbol{30^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{60^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{90^\\circ}$ triangles**.\n\nBased on the picture above, we see a special property shared by all $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles:\n\n> *In any ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the hypotenuse is twice as long as the shortest leg (which is opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle).*\n\nThis also works in the other direction:\n\n> *If the hypotenuse is twice as long as the shortest leg, then the given right triangle is a ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle.*" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186060874 1064 "Let's consider an equilateral triangle with sides of length $2x$. Notice that any altitude will split it into two congruent triangles, as shown below.\n\n![](images/30/301fe26a314d91d0d316758a5076cea2383f325991789addceaec71f0e7b16dd.png)\n\nIn the new smaller triangle the acute angles will be $30^\\circ$ and $60^\\circ.$ Such triangles are called **$\\boldsymbol{30^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{60^\\circ}$-$\\boldsymbol{90^\\circ}$ triangles**.\n\nBased on the picture above, we see a special property shared by all $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangles:\n\n*In any ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the hypotenuse is twice as long as the shortest leg (which is opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle).*\n\nThis also works in the other direction:\n\n*If the hypotenuse is twice as long as the shortest leg, then the given right triangle is a ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle.*" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186060875 1064 "Consider the same ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle from before. This time, however, we will focus on the height ($h$) instead of the hypotenuse.\n\n![](images/30/301fe26a314d91d0d316758a5076cea2383f325991789addceaec71f0e7b16dd.png)\n\nApplying the Pythagorean Theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x)^{2} &= x^{2} + h^{2} \\\\\nh^{2} &= 4x^{2} - x^{2} \\\\\nh^{2} &= 3x^{2} \\\\\nh &= \\sqrt{3}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the second important property:\n\n> *In any ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the length of the longer leg (which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle) is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the length of the shorter leg (which is opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle).*\n\nWe can summarize everything we know about ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangles using the diagram below:\n\n![](images/85/8598f9163b29833bb5895416e3dec9b6aa2358bd2cfcca944625ebb9f576b3a4.png)" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186060875 1064 "Consider the same ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle from before. This time, however, we will focus on the height ($h$) instead of the hypotenuse.\n\n![](images/30/301fe26a314d91d0d316758a5076cea2383f325991789addceaec71f0e7b16dd.png)\n\nApplying the Pythagorean Theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{h}^{2} \\\\ {h}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\ {h}^{2} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\sqrt{3}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the second important property:\n\n*In any ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangle, the length of the longer leg (which is opposite the $60^\\circ$ angle) is $\\sqrt{3}$ times the length of the shorter leg (which is opposite the $30^\\circ$ angle).*\n\nWe can summarize everything we know about ${30^\\circ}$-${60^\\circ}$-${90^\\circ}$ triangles using the diagram below:\n\n![](images/85/8598f9163b29833bb5895416e3dec9b6aa2358bd2cfcca944625ebb9f576b3a4.png)" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186061170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061171 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186061170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061172 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186061170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061173 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186061170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061174 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186061170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061175 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186061170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490808 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186061170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490843 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186061170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490848 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186061170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490948 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186061170 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490974 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186061170 1078 #edb/ref 17592186490808 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186062713 1078 #edb/ref 17592186062716 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186063947 1074 "First, remember that a fraction represents a division.  So, a fraction of fractions is a division of two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(-\\frac{3}{7})} & =\\frac{1}{4}÷(-\\frac{3}{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $-\\dfrac{3}{7}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $-\\dfrac{7}{3}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}÷(-\\frac{3}{7}) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\times (-\\frac{7}{3}) & = \\\\ -\\frac{1\\times 7}{4\\times 3} & = \\\\ -\\frac{7}{12} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(-\\frac{3}{7})} & =-\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the fact that a positive number times a negative number gives a negative number." 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186063947 1074 "First, remember that a fraction represents a division. So, a fraction of fractions is a division of two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((\\frac{1}{4}))/((-\\frac{3}{7})) &= \\frac{1}{4} \\div (-\\frac{3}{7})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{3}{7}\n$$\n\nis the same as multiplying by its reciprocal\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can use this fact to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{4} \\div (-\\frac{3}{7}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot (-\\frac{7}{3}) &=  \\\\\n- \\frac{1 \\cdot 7}{4 \\cdot 3} &=  \\\\\n&= - \\frac{7}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((\\frac{1}{4}))/((-\\frac{3}{7})) &= -\\frac{7}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the fact that a positive number times a negative number gives a negative number." 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186063948 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{\\left(-3\\right)} {\\left(-\\dfrac 3 5\\right)}$." 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186063948 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{\\left(-3\\right)} {\\left(-\\dfrac 3 5\\right)}.$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186063948 1074 "First, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n((-3))/((-\\frac{3}{5})) =-3 \\div (-\\frac{3}{5})\n$$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n-3 =-\\dfrac{3}{1}\n$$\n\nand dividing by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nis the same as multiplying by its reciprocal\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can use these facts to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-3) \\div (-\\frac{3}{5}) &=  \\\\\n(-\\frac{3}{1}) \\div (-\\frac{3}{5}) &=  \\\\\n(-\\frac{3}{1}) \\cdot (-\\frac{5}{3}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{3 \\cdot 5}{1 \\cdot 3} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{15}{3} &=  \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-3\\right)} {\\left(-\\dfrac 3 5\\right)} = 5\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the fact that a negative number times a negative number gives a positive number." 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186063948 1074 "First, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(-3)}{(-\\frac{3}{5})}=-3÷(-\\frac{3}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $-3 =- \\dfrac{3}{1}$ and dividing by $-\\dfrac{3}{5}$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $-\\dfrac{5}{3}.$ We can use these facts to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)÷(-\\frac{3}{5}) & = \\\\ (-\\frac{3}{1})÷(-\\frac{3}{5}) & = \\\\ (-\\frac{3}{1})\\times (-\\frac{5}{3}) & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\times 5}{1\\times 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{15}{3} & = \\\\ 5 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-3\\right)} {\\left(-\\dfrac 3 5\\right)} = 5.\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the fact that a negative number times a negative number gives a positive number." 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186063949 1071 "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6}$." 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186063949 1071 "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6}.$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186063949 1074 "We can use either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1 - The Long Way**\n\nFirst, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6} = \\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right) \\div 6\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $6$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can use this fact to simplify the fraction above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-\\frac{3}{2}) \\div 6 &=  \\\\\n(-\\frac{3}{2}) \\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}) &=  \\\\\n- \\frac{3 \\cdot 1}{2 \\cdot 6} &=  \\\\\n&= - \\frac{3}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{3}{12} =-\\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{12 \\div 3} = -\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6}= -\\dfrac14\n$$\n\n**Method 2 - The Quick Way**\n\nThe result will have the same numerator as the top fraction, and the denominator is just the product of the two denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((-\\frac{3}{2}))/(6) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{-3}{2 \\cdot 6} &=  \\\\\n- \\frac{3}{12} &=  \\\\\n- \\frac{3 \\div 3}{12 \\div 3} &=  \\\\\n&= - \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186063949 1074 "We can use either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1 - The Long Way** \n\nFirst, remember that a fraction represents a division:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{6} = \\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)  \\div 6\n$$\n\nNow, recall that dividing by $6$ is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal $\\dfrac{1}{6}.$ We can use this fact to simplify the fraction above: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{3}{2})÷6 & = \\\\ (-\\frac{