n{{field-1}}." 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186101260 1071 "Written in the form $y=mx+b,$ what is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(3,-10)$ and $(0,-4)?$\n\nThe equation of the line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186101260 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-(-10)}{0-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4+10}{0-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{-3} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,b).$ We know that the line passes through the point $(0,-4),$ so the $y$-intercept is $b=-4.$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-2$ and the $y$-intercept $b=-4$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=-2x-4.\n$$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186102020 1064 "The **slope-intercept** form of the equation of a line is\n\n$$\ny = {\\color{red}{m}}x+{\\color{blue}{b}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{red}{m}}$ is the **slope** of the line and ${\\color{blue}{b}}$ is the **$y$-intercept.**\n\nTo illustrate, consider the line\n\n$$\ny={\\color{red}{2}}x+{\\color{blue}{1}}\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4c1f53335d7637a0b08f51e93e760d533986e53a4c8896712262688b156bc70.png)\n\nLooking at the line, we notice that:\n\n- The *slope* of the line is $m={\\color{red}{2}}$, because a change of $1$ in the $x$-direction results in a change of $2$ in the $y$-direction.\n- The $y$*-intercept* of the line, denoted $b$, is $b={\\color{blue}{1}}$, because the line hits the $y$-axis at the point $(0,{\\color{blue}{1}})$.\n\n**Note**: The coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,{\\color{blue}{b}})$.\n\nFinally, if the slope $m = 0$ (i.e., we have a horizontal line), the slope-intercept form must be\n\n$$\ny=0x+{\\color{blue}b}\n$$\n\nwhich reduces to\n\n$$\ny={\\color{blue}b}\n$$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186102020 1064 "The **slope-intercept** form of the equation of a line is\n\n$$\ny = {\\color{red}{m}}x+{\\color{blue}{b}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{red}{m}}$ is the **slope** of the line and ${\\color{blue}{b}}$ is the **$y$-intercept.**\n\nTo illustrate, consider the line $y={\\color{red}{2}}x+{\\color{blue}{1}},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4c1f53335d7637a0b08f51e93e760d533986e53a4c8896712262688b156bc70.png)\n\nLooking at the line, we notice that:\n\n- The *slope* of the line is $m={\\color{red}{2}},$ because a change of $1$ in the $x$-direction results in a change of $2$ in the $y$-direction.\n\n- The $y$*-intercept* of the line, denoted $b$, is $b={\\color{blue}{1}},$ because the line hits the $y$-axis at the point $(0,{\\color{blue}{1}}).$\n\n**Note**: The coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,{\\color{blue}{b}}).$\n\nFinally, if the slope $m = 0$ (i.e., we have a horizontal line), the slope-intercept form must be $y=0x+{\\color{blue}b},$ which reduces to $y={\\color{blue}b}.$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186110625 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491120 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186110625 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491213 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186110626 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491116 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186110626 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491157 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186121826 1078 #edb/ref 17592186121829 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186124521 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491133 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186124631 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491115 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186124871 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186124871 1071 "Given $D = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 10 & 9 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -4 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$, what is $dim(Null(D))$?" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186124871 1071 "Given $D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 10 & 9 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -4 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,$ what is $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(D))?$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186124871 1074 "To find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(D))$, we reduce $D$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 10 & 9 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -4 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 2 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -4 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -4 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns and $2$ non-pivot columns (namely, the $3$rd and $5$th columns). \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(D))=2.\n$$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186144974 1078 #edb/ref 17592186144977 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186144986 1078 #edb/ref 17592186144989 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186147563 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491187 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186147595 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491195 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186147870 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186147870 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the weight of the concrete mix, which is $600$ kilograms. The percentage is $19.5%,$ and the part is the weight of the crushed stone in the mix.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n19.5%=19.5÷100=0.195\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =19.5%\\times 600 \\\\  & =0.195\\times 600 \\\\  & =117\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $19.5%$ of $600$ is $117.$ Thus, there are $117$ kilograms of crushed stone in the concrete mix.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n19.5%=\\frac{19.5}{100}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =19.5%\\times 600 \\\\  & =\\frac{19.5}{100}\\times 600 \\\\  & =19.5\\times \\frac{600}{100} \\\\  & =19.5\\times 6 \\\\  & =117\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $19.5%$ of $600$ is $117.$ Thus, there are $117$ kilograms of crushed stone in the concrete mix." 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186147872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186147872 1074 "To solve this problem, we can use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{part}=\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the whole is the amount of fabric Anna bought the first time, which is $15$ meters. The percentage is $160%,$ and the part is the amount of fabric she bought on the second purchase.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n160%=160÷100=1.6\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =160%\\times 15 \\\\  & =1.6\\times 15 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $160%$ of $15$ is $24.$ So, Anna bought $24$ meters of fabric on the second purchase.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we convert the percentage to a fraction:\n\n$$\n160%=\\frac{160}{100}=\\frac{160÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nApplying our formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{part} & =\\text{percentage}\\times \\text{whole} \\\\  & =160%\\times 15 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\times 15 \\\\  & =8\\times \\frac{15}{5} \\\\  & =8\\times 3 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $160%$ of $15$ is $24.$ So, Anna bought $24$ meters of fabric on the second purchase." 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186156105 1078 #edb/ref 17592186156107 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157118 1071 "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n- The number of times a die is rolled until we get a $4$ for the first time\n\n- The number of times a $2$ is rolled when a fair die is rolled $10$ times\n\n- The number of times a coin is tossed until we get a head" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186157118 1071 "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\nI. The number of times a die is rolled until we get a $4$ for the first time\n\nII. The number of times a $2$ is rolled when a fair die is rolled $10$ times\n\nIII. The number of times a coin is tossed until we get a head" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157120 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of matches until a tie is obtained. Since the chance of obtaining a tie in any match is $10\\%,$ the number of matches until a tie is obtained can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 10\\% = \\dfrac{10}{100} = 0.1.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.1).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.1)^{x-1}(0.1) \\\\  & =(0.9)^{x-1}(0.1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the team gets the first tie within the first $3$ matches is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.9)^{1-1}(0.1)+(0.9)^{2-1}(0.1)+(0.9)^{3-1}(0.1) \\\\  & =(0.1)+(0.9)(0.1)+(0.9)^{2}(0.1) \\\\  & =[1+0.9+(0.9)^{2}](0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.271\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157120 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of matches until a tie is obtained. Since the chance of obtaining a tie in any match is $10\\%,$ the number of matches until a tie is obtained can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 10\\% = \\dfrac{10}{100} = 0.1.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.1).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.1)^{x-1}(0.1) \\\\  & =(0.9)^{x-1}(0.1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the team gets the first tie within the first $3$ matches is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.9)^{1-1}(0.1)+(0.9)^{2-1}(0.1)+(0.9)^{3-1}(0.1) \\\\  & =(0.1)+(0.9)(0.1)+(0.9)^{2}(0.1) \\\\  & =[1+0.9+(0.9)^{2}](0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.271\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186157121 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears. Since the chance of finding an applicant who meets all the requirements is $35\\%,$ the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 35\\% = \\dfrac{35}{100} = 0.35.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.35).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.35)^{x-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{x-1}(0.35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X \\gt 3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X \\gt 3) = 1 - P(X \\leq  3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{1-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{2-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{3-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.35)+(0.65)(0.35)+(0.65)^{2}(0.35) \\\\  & =[1+0.65+(0.65)^{2}](0.35) \\\\  & ≈0.725\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that an applicant who meets all the requirements will appear for the first time after the $3$rd interviewee is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & ≈1-0.725 \\\\  & ≈0.275\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157121 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears. Since the chance of finding an applicant who meets all the requirements is $35\\%,$ the number of applicants interviewed until an applicant who meets all requirements appears can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np = 35\\% = \\dfrac{35}{100} = 0.35.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(0.35).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.35)^{x-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{x-1}(0.35)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X \\gt 3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X \\gt 3) = 1 - P(X \\leq  3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.65)^{1-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{2-1}(0.35)+(0.65)^{3-1}(0.35) \\\\  & =(0.35)+(0.65)(0.35)+(0.65)^{2}(0.35) \\\\  & =[1+0.65+(0.65)^{2}](0.35) \\\\  & ≈0.725\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that an applicant who meets all the requirements will appear for the first time after the $3$rd interviewee is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & ≈1-0.725 \\\\  & ≈0.275\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186157314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491174 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491178 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491200 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157314 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491215 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491124 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491132 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491142 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491155 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491176 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491134 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491138 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491139 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491161 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491197 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157317 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491148 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157317 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491209 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157494 1050 720.0 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157864 1071 "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\n1. The number of questions answered correctly on a quiz before the first incorrect answer\n2. The cost of an item randomly selected from a store\n3. The number of products checked before finding a defective product" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186157864 1071 "Which of the following random variables follow a geometric distribution?\n\nI. The number of questions answered correctly on a quiz before the first incorrect answer\n\nII. The cost of an item randomly selected from a store\n\nIII. The number of products checked before finding a defective product" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186157864 1074 "A geometric distribution is used to model the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a geometric distribution. Answering a question correctly (or incorrectly) is a binomial experiment, and the number of questions answered before the first incorrect answer can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow a geometric distribution. The cost of an item randomly selected from a store is not a binomial experiment.\n\n- Random variable III follows a geometric distribution. Checking a product is a binomial experiment, and the number of products checked before the first defective product is found can be interpreted as the number of binomial experiments needed before the first success.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186159008 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159011 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186160874 1078 #edb/ref 17592186160878 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186160988 1078 #edb/ref 17592186160993 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186161776 1071 "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2?$\n\n![](images/52/528416f6f9bd58e04afc6df73ddffbe1e8645f1e94fc7113d1458fb806f08713.svg)" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186161776 1071 "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on $\\mathbb{R}^2?$\n\n![](images/e5/e51c52e07d49052f578138e5c1d7e9f84a95ba8c454e7fcf834dcc9047dad432.png)" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false