 17592186045417 true] [17592186161985 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491171 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186161987 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491163 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162069 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491208 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162107 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491194 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162108 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491123 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162108 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491183 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162108 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491203 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491140 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162157 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491114 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162157 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491151 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162158 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491137 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162219 1050 540.0 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162301 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>Y).$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162301 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n$f(x, y) = {1,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nFind $P(X > Y)$." 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186162301 1074 "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,1]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A=\\{X>Y\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/02/023e458b0d61dbb9b4bd475618db63c565abf3cf15bcb7cfc3afd52217b66426.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>Y) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{A}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162597 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162597 1071 "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ b & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}\\,$ is given above. What is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162597 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ b & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe matrix of the quadratic form $Q(x) = x_{1}^{2} + 8x_{1}x_{2} + x_{2}^{2}$ is given above. What is the value of $a + b$?" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186162597 1074 "Since the matrix of the quadratic form must be symmetric, we rewrite our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =1{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & +\\,4{x}_{2}{x}_{1}+1{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that to get a symmetric matrix, we must split the coefficient of $8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ between the entries \n\n$$\n{a}_{12}={a}_{21}=\\frac{8}{2}=4\n$$\n\nof our matrix. So, the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1$ and $b=4.$ Finally, \n\n$$\na+b=1+4=5.\n$$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162616 1071 "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on $R^{3}$?\n\n$H = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix}, K = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186162616 1071 "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186162616 1074 "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $H$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $J$ could also be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $K$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}.$ Although it's a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $K$ is not symmetric since ${k}_{23}\\ne {k}_{32}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$H$ and $J$ only.\"" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186163226 1064 "Given a bilinear form $B(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})$ such that \n\n$$\nB:\\Bbb R^n \\times \\Bbb R^n \\to \\Bbb R\n$$\n\nand the corresponding $n \\times n$ matrix is *symmetric*, we can define the so-called **quadratic form** by substituting $\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= B(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{x}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the new expression depends only on $\\mathbf{x}.$ So, a quadratic form is a function \n\n$$\nQ:\\Bbb R^n \\to \\Bbb R.\n$$\n\nIn matrix notation, a quadratic form  can be given as \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $A$ is a symmetric matrix. It's called the **matrix of the quadratic form**. \n\n**Watch out!** By definition, the matrix of a quadratic form must be *symmetric*.\n\nFor example, given the bilinear form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nthe corresponding quadratic form is then\n$Q( \\mathbf{x})=B( \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{x}).$\nExplicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Note**: In a quadratic form, all terms must be of degree two (considered as polynomials in the variables $x_1, x_2, \\cdots, x_n$). That's the reason we call it *quadratic*." 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186163226 1064 "Given a bilinear form $B(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})$ such that \n\n$$\nB:\\Bbb R^n \\times \\Bbb R^n \\to \\Bbb R\n$$\n\nand the corresponding $n \\times n$ matrix is *symmetric*, we can define the so-called **quadratic form** by substituting $\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= B(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{x}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the new expression depends only on $\\mathbf{x}.$ So, a quadratic form is a function \n\n$$\nQ:\\Bbb R^n \\to \\Bbb R.\n$$\n\nIn matrix notation, a quadratic form  can be given as \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $A$ is a symmetric matrix. It's called the **matrix of the quadratic form**. \n\n**Watch out!** By definition, the matrix of a quadratic form must be *symmetric*.\n\nFor example, given the bilinear form \n\n![](images/99/996c4aa136dd7cda56787a2edb0f9481ed3d9356248a27afcde050ca613b6b9c.svg) \n\nthe corresponding quadratic form is then\n$Q( \\mathbf{x})=B( \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{x}).$\nExplicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n**Note**: In a quadratic form, all terms must be of degree two (considered as polynomials in the variables $x_1, x_2, \\cdots, x_n$). That's the reason we call it *quadratic*." 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186163552 1078 #edb/ref 17592186163553 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186166852 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491156 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186166856 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491204 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186166856 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491211 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186166868 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491129 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186166868 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491153 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186167079 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186167079 1071 "Given that $X∼Exp(\\frac{5}{6})$, what is $SD[X]$?" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186167079 1071 "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{5}{6}),$ what is $\\text{SD}[X]?$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186167079 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]}=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{5}{6}),$ we have the following standard deviation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186173740 1078 #edb/ref 17592186173742 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186175552 1078 #edb/ref 17592186175557 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186175946 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491127 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186175984 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491198 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186175993 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491152 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186175993 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491199 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186176150 1050 420.0 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186176247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186176247 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{3}(4-\\lambda )^{2}(7-\\lambda )^{6},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D,$ how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0?$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186176247 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $D$ has characteristic polynomial $p(λ) = λ^{3}(4 - λ)^{2}(7 - λ)^{6}$, and for the eigenvalue $λ = 0$, there are exactly $2$ eigenvectors. If a basis of $R^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $D$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $λ = 0$?" 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186176247 1074 "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{3}(4-\\lambda )^{2}(7-\\lambda )^{6},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =0$ has multiplicity $3.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $3.$\n\nHowever, there are only $2$ eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $2.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =0$ is $3-2=1.$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186176251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186173764 13194139535285 17592186045417 false] [17592186176569 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186176569 1071 "